---
import "../../styles/global.css";
import BaseHead from "../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead
      title="Gelatin Reference Articles | gelatincalculator.com"
      description="Plain-English guides on gelatin bloom strength, sheet-to-powder conversion, and how much gelatin to use per cup of liquid."
      path="/articles/"
    />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-white text-stone-900">
    <nav class="border-b border-stone-200">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-5 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
        <a href="/" class="font-serif text-xl text-stone-900">gelatincalculator.com</a>
        <div class="flex gap-5 text-sm text-stone-600">
          <a href="/about" class="hover:text-amber-700">About</a>
          <a href="/privacy" class="hover:text-amber-700">Privacy</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <main class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-5 py-16">
      <h1 class="font-serif text-5xl text-stone-900 mb-4">Articles</h1>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Almost every gelatin problem a home cook runs into comes down to one
        thing: the recipe was written for a gelatin that is not the gelatin
        you have. A French pastry book assumes sheets of a particular grade.
        An American recipe assumes a supermarket powder. Neither one tells
        you what to do when you are holding the other, and consumer packaging
        almost never prints the number that would let you work it out for
        yourself.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-12">
        These pages exist to close that gap. They are written to be read
        standing in a kitchen rather than studied — short, specific, and
        honest about which figures are firmly established and which are
        reasonable estimates. Start with whichever question below is
        actually yours.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-stone-900 mb-3">Understanding bloom strength</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Bloom strength is the number that makes gelatin substitutable. If you
        only read one thing on this site before using the calculator, make it
        one of these two pages. They cover the same concept from different
        angles: the first is the more technical explanation, the second is
        organised around what you are actually trying to make.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-4 mb-12">
        <a href="/what-is-gelatin-bloom-strength/" class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-amber-300 transition-colors">
          <p class="font-serif text-xl text-stone-900 mb-2">What Is Gelatin Bloom Strength?</p>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Answers the question "what does this number on a professional
            recipe actually mean, and why does it change how much gelatin I
            need?" It defines the Bloom test itself, lays out the sheet grade
            scale from bronze through platinum in a table giving the bloom
            range, relative set strength and typical uses of each grade, and then
            explains why two gelatins of the same weight do not set the same
            way. It also addresses Knox and generic supermarket powder
            specifically, and why consumer packaging leaves the value off.
            Read this one if you want to understand the mechanism rather than
            just get a number.
          </p>
        </a>

        <a href="/what-is-bloom-strength/" class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-amber-300 transition-colors">
          <p class="font-serif text-xl text-stone-900 mb-2">Gelatin Bloom Strength — Plain-English Guide</p>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            The same subject approached from the recipe end. It opens with a
            short answer for anyone who does not want the theory, gives a
            table of common products and their bloom values by form, and then
            works through which strength suits which dish — panna cotta,
            gummies, mirror glaze, marshmallow, stabilised whipped cream.
            The most useful section is the last one: what to do when the
            gelatin in your cupboard does not match the one the recipe was
            written for. The FAQ also covers agar agar, which behaves
            differently enough that bloom values do not carry over to it.
          </p>
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-stone-900 mb-3">Sheets, powder, and converting between them</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Sheets and powder are the same ingredient sold in two shapes, and the
        difference between them causes more failed sets than any other single
        factor. These two pages cover the comparison and the conversion.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-4 mb-12">
        <a href="/gelatin-sheets-vs-powder/" class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-amber-300 transition-colors">
          <p class="font-serif text-xl text-stone-900 mb-2">Gelatin Sheets vs Powder: What's the Difference?</p>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            For anyone deciding which format to buy, or trying to work out why
            a recipe insists on one. A side-by-side table compares the two on how
            each is measured, the bloom grades each is sold in, how each is
            dissolved, clarity and flavour in the finished dish, and which
            cooking traditions favour which. The page then explains what
            happens when a recipe written for one format is cooked with the
            other. It is the right starting point if you have
            inherited a recipe that calls for sheets and have only ever used
            Knox, or the reverse.
          </p>
        </a>

        <a href="/sheets-to-powder/" class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-amber-300 transition-colors">
          <p class="font-serif text-xl text-stone-900 mb-2">Gelatin Sheets to Powder Conversion</p>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            The one page here with a working tool on it. Enter a sheet grade and
            a number of sheets and it returns the equivalent weight of powder;
            a toggle runs the same conversion in reverse, from a weight of
            powder to a count of sheets. The prose around the converter explains why there is no
            single sheet-to-powder rule that holds — grades differ in both
            bloom strength and sheet weight, and ignoring either one throws
            the result off. Worked scenarios cover the grade and quantity
            combinations that come up most often. Use this when you have a
            recipe in sheets and a jar of powder.
          </p>
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-stone-900 mb-3">Getting the quantity right</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4 mb-12">
        <a href="/how-much-gelatin-per-cup-of-liquid/" class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-amber-300 transition-colors">
          <p class="font-serif text-xl text-stone-900 mb-2">How Much Gelatin Per Cup of Liquid?</p>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            For the situation where you have no recipe at all, only a liquid
            and an idea of the texture you want. A table gives quantities per
            cup for four target textures, from a soft set through to very
            firm, in both powder and sheets. The rest of the page is
            about what moves those numbers: acidity, sugar load, dairy, and
            the proteolytic enzymes in fresh pineapple and papaya that will
            stop a set from happening at all. A closing section deals with
            temperature, which is the other half of the same problem.
          </p>
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 p-5 bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl">
        <p class="font-medium text-stone-900 mb-2">Not sure what bloom strength your gelatin is?</p>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-3">
          The main calculator starts with a diagnostic step — answer a few questions about
          your gelatin and it identifies the likely bloom value before running the conversion.
        </p>
        <a href="/" class="text-amber-700 underline underline-offset-4 text-sm hover:text-amber-600">
          Go to the calculator →
        </a>
      </div>
    </main>

    <Footer />
  </body>
</html>
